:8+7=15 \u21925, carry1\n\nPosition8:9+5=14 +1=15 \u21925, carry1\n\nPosition9:1+9=10 +1=11 \u21921, carry1\n\nNow, after Position9, we have a carryover of 1. So the total digits are 10, with the first digit being 1 followed by the rest.\n\nSo putting all together from last to first (since we built it from right to left):\n\nThe digits from Position1 to Position9 plus the final carryover:\n\nWait, actually, the carryover after Position9 is 1, so the digits would be:\n\nCarryover 1, then the result of Position9 (1), then Position8 (5), etc. Wait, perhaps I need to list them in order.\n\nWait, let me think again. The positions are numbered from right (ones) to left (hundred millions). The carryover after the hundred millions (position9) is 1, so that becomes the next digit (billions place). So the digits from right to left are:\n\nPosition1:4\n\nPosition2:4\n\nPosition3:5\n\nPosition4:3\n\nPosition5:7\n\nPosition6:6\n\nPosition7:5\n\nPosition8:5\n\nPosition9:1\n\nPlus the final carryover 1 at the beginning (billions place).\n\nSo arranging them from left to right (billions down to ones):\n\nStarting with the carryover 1 (billions place), then the Position9 digit (1), Position8 (5), Position7 (5), Position6 (6), Position5 (7), Position4 (3), Position3 (5), Position2 (4), Position1 (4).\n\nWait, let me list them step by step:\n\nThe final digits after each position:\n\nAfter Position1: 4 (ones)\n\nAfter Position2: 4 (tens)\n\nAfter Position3:5 (hundreds)\n\nAfter Position4:3 (thousands)\n\nAfter Position5:7 (ten thousands)\n\nAfter Position6:6 (hundred thousands)\n\nAfter Position7:5 (millions)\n\nAfter Position8:5 (ten millions)\n\nAfter Position9:1 (hundred millions)\n\nPlus the carryover 1 (billions place).\n\nSo the digits from highest to lowest (billions to ones):\n\nBillions:1\n\nHundred millions:1\n\nTen millions:5\n\nMillions:5\n\nHundred thousands:6\n\nTen thousands:7\n\nThousands:3\n\nHundreds:5\n\nTens:4\n\nOnes:4\n\nWait, that might not be right. Let me structure it properly.\n\nThe positions after adding:\n\nThe first digit (rightmost) is the ones place:4\n\nThen tens:4\n\nHundreds:5\n\nThousands:3\n\nTen thousands:7\n\nHundred thousands:6\n\nMillions:5\n\nTen millions:5\n\nHundred millions:1 (from Position9's result) plus the carryover 1 from Position9? Wait, no. Wait, the Position9 calculation was 1 (from first number's hundred millions) +9 (second's hundred millions) + carryover1 from Position8's addition (which was 15, so carryover1). So 1+9+1=11. So the digit in the hundred millions place is 1 (the 1 from 11), and carryover1 to the next (billions) place. So the billions place is that 1.\n\nTherefore, the digits from left (billions) to right (ones):\n\nBillions:1\n\nHundred millions:1 (from the 11 in Position9)\n\nTen millions:5 (from Position8's result)\n\nMillions:5 (Position7)\n\nHundred thousands:6 (Position6)\n\nTen thousands:7 (Position5)\n\nThousands:3 (Position4)\n\nHundreds:5 (Position3)\n\nTens:4 (Position2)\n\nOnes:4 (Position1)\n\nSo putting all together:\n\n1 (billions), 1 (hundred millions), 5 (ten millions), 5 (millions), 6 (hundred thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands), 5 (hundreds), 4 (tens), 4 (ones).\n\nSo the number is 1,155,673,544?\n\nWait, let me check that:\n\nBreaking down:\n\nBillions:1 \u2192 1,000,000,000\n\nHundred millions:1 \u2192 100,000,000 \u2192 total so far 1,100,000,000\n\nTen millions:5 \u2192 50,000,000 \u2192 1,150,000,000\n\nMillions:5 \u2192 5,000,000 \u2192 1,155,000,000\n\nHundred thousands:6 \u2192 600,000 \u2192 1,155,600,000\n\nTen thousands:7 \u219270,000 \u21921,155,670,000\n\nThousands:3 \u21923,000 \u21921,155,673,000\n\nHundreds:5 \u2192500 \u21921,155,673,500\n\nTens:4 \u219240 \u21921,155,673,540\n\nOnes:4 \u2192 total 1,155,673,544.\n\nHmm, but let me verify with another method. Maybe adding the numbers directly:\n\n198,356,259\n\n+957,317,285\n\nLet me add them step by step:\n\nFirst, adding the two numbers:\n\n198,356,259\n\n+957,317,285\n\nStart from the right:\n\n9 +5 =14 \u21924, carry1\n\n5+8=13 +1=14 \u21924, carry1\n\n2+2=4 +1=5 \u21925, carry0\n\n6+7=13 \u21923, carry1\n\n5+1=6 +1=7 \u21927, carry0\n\n3+3=6 \u21926, carry0\n\n8+7=15 \u21925, carry1\n\n9+5=14 +1=15 \u21925, carry1\n\n1+9=10 +1=11 \u21921, carry1\n\nThen the final carryover 1.\n\nSo the result is 1,155,673,544. That seems correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can check using another approach, like rounding:\n\n198,356,259 is approximately 200,000,000\n\n957,317,285 is approximately 957,000,000\n\nAdding those gives about 1,157,000,000. The actual result is 1,155,673,544, which is a bit less, which makes sense because the first number was rounded up by about 1.6 million, and the second was rounded down by about 317 thousand. The difference would be roughly 1.6M - 0.317M \u22481.28M, so subtracting that from 1,157M gives around 1,155.7M, which matches the exact result. So that seems plausible.\n\nTherefore, the final answer should be 1,155,673,544.\n</think>\n\n",
