ain carefully.\n\nStarting over:\n\nOnes: 6 +7 =13. Write 3, carry 1.\n\nTens:5+5+1=11. Write 1, carry 1.\n\nHundreds:8+5+1=14. Write 4, carry 1.\n\nThousands:4+5+1=10. Write 0, carry 1.\n\nTen-thousands:6+1+1=8 (since the next digit from the second number is 1 in the ten-thousands place? Wait, let me check the original numbers again.\n\nWait, maybe I misaligned the digits. Let me confirm the numbers:\n\n364,856 is 3 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 6 (ones).\n\n915,557 is 9 (hundred thousands), 1 (ten thousands), 5 (thousands), 5 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 7 (ones).\n\nSo aligning properly:\n\nHundred thousands: 3 and 9\n\nTen thousands: 6 and 1\n\nThousands:4 and5\n\nHundreds:8 and5\n\nTens:5 and5\n\nOnes:6 and7.\n\nWait, so when adding the ten-thousands place: 6 (from first number) and 1 (from second number) plus the carried over 1 from thousands place addition. Wait, let's go step by step again.\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes: 6+7=13 \u2192 3, carryover 1.\n\nTens:5+5=10 +1=11 \u21921, carryover1.\n\nHundreds:8+5=13 +1=14 \u21924, carryover1.\n\nThousands:4+5=9 +1=10 \u21920, carryover1.\n\nTen-thousands:6 (from first number's ten-thousands place) +1 (from second number's ten-thousands place) +1 (carryover) = 8. So that's 8, no carryover here?\n\nWait, 6+1 is 7, plus 1 is 8. So yes, 8 with no carryover. Then the hundred-thousands place: 3 +9 =12. So that's 2 in the hundred-thousands and carryover 1 to the millions place. So the total would be 1 (million) 2 (hundred thousand) 8 (ten thousand) 0 (thousand) 4 (hundred) 1 (ten) 3 (one). So putting it all together: 1,280,413?\n\nWait, let me check with another method. Maybe adding the numbers in parts.\n\n364,856 + 915,557.\n\nLet me break it down:\n\n364,856 + 900,000 = 1,264,856\n\nThen add 15,557: 1,264,856 +15,557.\n\n1,264,856 +10,000 =1,274,856\n\nThen +5,557: 1,274,856 +5,000 =1,279,856; then +557 \u21921,280,413. Okay, that matches. So the answer should be 1,280,413.\n\nWait, but in the initial step-by-step addition, when I added the hundred thousands place, 3+9=12, so that would be 2 in the hundred thousands and carryover 1 to millions, making it 1,2... So the total is indeed 1,280,413. Okay, that seems right. Let me confirm once more with another approach.\n\nAlternatively, adding digit by digit again:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes:6+7=13 \u21923, carryover1.\n\nTens:5+5=10 +1=11 \u21921, carryover1.\n\nHundreds:8+5=13 +1=14 \u21924, carryover1.\n\nThousands:4+5=9 +1=10 \u21920, carryover1.\n\nTen-thousands:6+1=7 +1=8 \u21928, no carryover.\n\nHundred-thousands:3+9=12 \u21922, carryover1.\n\nMillions place: the carryover 1.\n\nSo writing all digits: 1 (million), 2 (hundred thousand), 8 (ten thousand), 0 (thousand), 4 (hundred), 1 (ten), 3 (one). So 1,280,413. Yes, that's correct. I think that's the right answer. I don't see any mistakes in the steps now.\n</think>\n\n",
