ousands 5, hundred thousands 8. Wait, that would make the sum 855,785? Let me verify that again because sometimes when adding, especially with multiple digits, it's easy to miscount the places.\n\nWait a second, maybe I made a mistake in the thousands place. Let me recount each step carefully.\n\nStarting over:\n\nFirst number: 435,317\nSecond number: 420,468\n\nBreaking down each digit:\n\n- Ones: 7 + 8 = 15 \u2192 5 with carryover 1\n- Tens: 1 (from first number) + 6 (from second) + 1 (carry) = 8 \u2192 no carryover\n- Hundreds: 3 (from first) + 4 (from second) = 7 \u2192 no carryover\n- Thousands: 5 (from first) + 0 (from second) = 5 \u2192 no carryover\n- Ten thousands: 3 (from first) + 2 (from second) = 5 \u2192 no carryover\n- Hundred thousands: 4 + 4 = 8 \u2192 no carryover\n\nSo the total is indeed 855,785. Wait, but let me add them numerically to confirm:\n\n435,317\n+420,468\n= ?\n\nAdding 435,317 + 400,000 = 835,317. Then add 20,468: 835,317 + 20,000 = 855,317, then +468 gives 855,785. Yes, that matches. So the answer should be 855,785. Okay, that seems right. I think that's correct.\n</think>\n\n",
