      "text": "\nOkay, let's tackle this addition problem. The question is asking for the sum of 876,474 and 637,395. Hmm, I need to add these two numbers together. Let me start by writing them down one on top of the other, aligning the digits properly by their place values. \n\nStarting from the rightmost digit, which is the ones place. The first number has a 4 in the ones place, and the second number has a 5 there. Adding those gives 4 + 5 = 9. So the ones place of the sum is 9. No carry-over here since 9 is a single digit.\n\nMoving to the tens place next. The first number has a 7, and the second has a 9. 7 + 9 equals 16. Oh, that's two digits, so I need to write down 6 in the tens place and carry over 1 to the hundreds place. \n\nNow the hundreds place. The first number has a 4, the second has a 3. Adding those gives 4 + 3 = 7, but don't forget the carried-over 1. So 7 + 1 = 8. So the hundreds digit is 8. No carry-over this time.\n\nNext up is the thousands place. The first number has a 6, and the second has a 7. 6 + 7 is 13. So we put 3 in the thousands place and carry over 1 again.\n\nMoving to the ten-thousands place. The first number has a 7, and the second has a 3. Adding those gives 7 + 3 = 10, plus the carried-over 1 makes 11. So we write 1 in the ten-thousands place and carry over another 1.\n\nNow the hundred-thousands place. The first number has an 8, and the second has a 6. 8 + 6 = 14, plus the carried-over 1 equals 15. So we write 5 in the hundred-thousands place and carry over 1 to the millions place. Since there are no more digits left, that carried-over 1 becomes the millions digit.\n\nPutting it all together, the sum should be 1,513,869. Wait, let me check again to make sure I didn't mix up any digits. Let me add them another way to verify. Maybe breaking them into parts. \n\n876,474 + 600,000 is 1,476,474. Then adding 37,395 (since 637,395 - 600,000 = 37,395). Adding 37,395 to 1,476,474: 1,476,474 + 30,000 = 1,506,474. Then +7,395: 1,506,474 + 7,000 = 1,513,474, then +395 gives 1,513,869. Okay, that matches. So the answer should be correct.\n</think>\n\n",
