ginal numbers again.\n\nWait, 927,036 is a six-digit number: 9 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 7 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 6 (ones). The second number, 320,153 is also six digits: 3 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 0 (thousands), 1 (hundred), 5 (tens), 3 (ones). So when adding, the highest place is hundred thousands. So adding 9 + 3 gives 12. Therefore, the sum will have a 2 in the hundred thousands place and carry over 1 to the millions place. But since there are no digits beyond hundred thousands, that carried-over 1 becomes a 1 in the millions place. So the total sum will be a seven-digit number starting with 1, followed by 2 in the hundred thousands, etc.\n\nPutting it all together, let me reconstruct each digit step by step:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes: 9\n\nTens: 8\n\nHundreds: 1\n\nThousands: 7\n\nTen thousands: 4\n\nHundred thousands: 2 (with a carryover of 1 to millions)\n\nMillions: 1 (from the carryover)\n\nSo combining all these digits: 1 (millions), 2 (hundred thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 7 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 8 (tens), 9 (ones). That makes the number 1,247,189.\n\nWait, let me verify that again to make sure I didn't make a mistake. Let me add the numbers another way to cross-check.\n\nAlternatively, adding the numbers step by step:\n\n927,036 + 320,153.\n\nBreaking it down:\n\nFirst, add the hundred thousands: 900,000 + 300,000 = 1,200,000.\n\nThen the ten thousands: 20,000 + 20,000 = 40,000.\n\nThousands: 7,000 + 0 = 7,000.\n\nHundreds: 0 + 100 = 100.\n\nTens: 30 + 50 = 80.\n\nOnes: 6 + 3 = 9.\n\nAdding all these components together:\n\n1,200,000 + 40,000 = 1,240,000.\n\n1,240,000 + 7,000 = 1,247,000.\n\n1,247,000 + 100 = 1,247,100.\n\n1,247,100 + 80 = 1,247,180.\n\n1,247,180 + 9 = 1,247,189.\n\nOkay, that matches the previous result. So the sum should be 1,247,189.\n\nWait, but let me do the addition the standard column method again to confirm.\n\nLet me write it out vertically again:\n\n```\n   927036\n+ 32