 number) + 8 (from second) = 14. Write 4, carryover 1.\n\nHundred-thousands place: 2 (from first) + 8 (from second) = 10, plus the carried-over 1 makes 11. So here, we have 11. Since this is the highest place, we write both digits, so it becomes 1,1 in the hundred-thousands and ten-thousands? Wait, maybe I need to adjust the places. Wait, actually, the hundred-thousands place is the sixth digit from the right. Let me see:\n\nWait, the first number is 266,665 which is 2 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 5 (ones).\n\nThe second number is 880,147 which is 8 (hundred thousands), 8 (ten thousands), 0 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 4 (tens), 7 (ones).\n\nAdding them:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes: 5+7=12 \u2192 2, carryover 1.\n\nTens: 6+4=10 +1=11 \u2192 1, carryover 1.\n\nHundreds: 6+1=7 +1=8 \u2192 8, no carryover.\n\nThousands: 6+0=6 \u2192 6.\n\nTen thousands: 6 (from first number's ten thousands place) + 8 (from second's ten thousands place) = 14 \u2192 4, carryover 1.\n\nHundred thousands: 2 +8 =10 +1 (carryover) =11 \u2192 11.\n\nSo putting all together, the digits from right to left are:\n\nOnes:2, Tens:1, Hundreds:8, Thousands:6, Ten-thousands:4, Hundred-thousands:11.\n\nWait, but how do we write 11 in the hundred-thousands place? That would actually make it 1,146,812? Wait, let me see:\n\nWait, the digits would be:\n\nThe hundred-thousands place is 11, so that's actually 1 million and 1 hundred thousand. So the total would be 1,146,812?\n\nWait, let me add them numerically:\n\n266,665 + 880,147.\n\nLet me compute this another way to verify.\n\n266,665 + 800,000 is 1,066,665. Then add 80,147: 1,066,665 + 80,147 = ?\n\n1,066,665 + 80,000 = 1,146,665. Then add 147: 1,146,665 + 147 = 1,146,812. Okay, so that matches. So the final answer is 1,146,812.\n\nWait, but in the step-by-step digit addition, when we had the hundred-thousands place as 11, that's 1,1 followed by the rest. So the digits would be 1 (millions place), 1 (hundred thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 1 (tens), 2 (ones). So that's 1,146,812. Yes, that's correct.\n\nSo the reasoning steps should be:\n\n1. Ones place: 5 + 7 = 12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\n2. Tens place: 6 + 4 = 10 + 1 = 11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\n3. Hundreds place: 6 + 1 = 7 + 1 = 8. Write 8.\n\n4. Thousands place: 6 + 0 = 6. Write 6.\n\n5. Ten-thousands place: 6 + 8 = 14. Write 4, carryover 1.\n\n6. Hundred-thousands place: 2 + 8 = 10 + 1 = 11. Write 11.\n\nSo combining all digits: 1,146,812.\n\nTherefore, the answer is correct.\n</think>\n\n",
